<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bohemia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bohemia is a historical region in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> forming the western part of the present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> as its centre. It was a kingdom within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, whose king was one of the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> it became one of the most industrialised parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bohemia.jpg" alt="Bohemia" width="320" height="246" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ввласенко · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl%C5%A1tejn_Castle,_Czech_Republic.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire was a mosaic of peoples: Germans, Hungarians, Czechs, Slovaks, Poles, Ruthenians, Romanians, Croats, Serbs, Slovenes and Italians, with large Jewish communities in Galicia, <b>Bohemia</b> and Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the industrialised provinces of <b>Bohemia</b> and Moravia, the Škoda works at Plzeň, textile mills, sugar refineries and glassworks made the region one of the most productive industrial zones in continental Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Frontiers were fixed only after fighting: troops occupied the German-speaking borderlands of <b>Bohemia</b> and Moravia, defended Slovakia against the Hungarian Soviet Republic in 1919 and clashed with Poland over Teschen, which was divided in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Hamburg lived from trade and from the Elbe, which connected it to Brandenburg, Saxony and <b>Bohemia</b> upstream and to the North Sea downstream.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its dynastic union with Poland, begun at Krewo in 1385, produced the Jagiellonian dynasty, which at times ruled Poland, Lithuania, <b>Bohemia</b> and Hungary, and ultimately the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…lasted only about 22 years in Moravia, the disciples expelled in 886 carried it to Bulgaria, Croatia and <b>Bohemia</b>; at the Preslav Literary School Bulgarian disciples such as Naum and Clement of Ohrid created the Cyrillic alphabet, which became the script of the Bulgarian Empire and Kievan Rus',…</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
